Q: Linq query io.DrpString.Contains(dienstregelpunt)&#39; could not be translated A: <p>As I already have mentioned, this issue is solved in EF v8 and later.</p>
<p>However if for any reason you cannot upgrate to that version, below are suggestions how to implement your requirements.</p>
<p>The other answer joins all conditions with <code>AND</code>, which would be equivalent if you used</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>dienstregelpunten.All(...
</code></pre>
<p>For <code>Any</code>, <code>OR</code> logic should be applied.</p>
<p>Below is sample implementation for that:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>using var _btdContext = new TestDbContext();

var dienstregelpunten = new string[] { &quot;ab&quot;, &quot;cd&quot; };

var query = _btdContext.tblOnttrekkingIncidenteel
    .Where(io =&gt; !string.IsNullOrEmpty(io.DrpString));

// Create predicate
Expression&lt;Func&lt;TblOnttrekkingIncidenteel, bool&gt;&gt; predicate = io =&gt; false;
predicate = dienstregelpunten.Aggregate(
    predicate,
    (current, p) =&gt; Or(
        current,
        io =&gt; io.DrpString.Contains(p)
    )
);

query = query.Where(predicate);

// inspect the query, to try out directly in SQL Server Management Studio
Console.WriteLine(query.ToQueryString());

var results = await query.ToArrayAsync();

// Helper method to combine two expressions with OR
static Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; Or&lt;T&gt;(
    Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; expr1,
    Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; expr2)
{
    var parameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T));

    var body = Expression.OrElse(
        Expression.Invoke(expr1, parameter),
        Expression.Invoke(expr2, parameter)
    );

    return Expression.Lambda&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt;(body, parameter);
}
</code></pre>
<p>You could define equivalent method also for <code>AND</code>.</p>
<p>Then on top of these method you could even define nice extension methods, which would allow nice syntax:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>var query = _btdContext.tblOnttrekkingIncidenteel
    .Where(io =&gt; !string.IsNullOrEmpty(io.DrpString))
    .WhereOr(
        dienstregelpunten, 
        (entity, d) =&gt; entity.DrpString.Contains(d));
</code></pre>
<p>Extension methods:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public static class QueryableExtensions
{
    public static IQueryable&lt;TSource&gt; WhereOr&lt;TSource, TCompare&gt;(
        this IQueryable&lt;TSource&gt; source,
        TCompare[] compareValues,
        Expression&lt;Func&lt;TSource, TCompare, bool&gt;&gt; predicate)
    {
        if (compareValues == null || compareValues.Length == 0)
            return source;

        var finalPredicate = compareValues.Skip(1).Aggregate(
            BindSecondParameter(predicate, compareValues[0]),
            (combined, next) =&gt; combined.Or(
                BindSecondParameter(predicate, next)));

        return source.Where(finalPredicate);
    }

    public static IQueryable&lt;TSource&gt; WhereAnd&lt;TSource, TCompare&gt;(
        this IQueryable&lt;TSource&gt; source,
        TCompare[] compareValues,
        Expression&lt;Func&lt;TSource, TCompare, bool&gt;&gt; predicate)
    {
        if (compareValues == null || compareValues.Length == 0)
            return source;

        var finalPredicate = compareValues.Skip(1).Aggregate(
            BindSecondParameter(predicate, compareValues[0]),
            (combined, next) =&gt; combined.And(
                BindSecondParameter(predicate, next)));

        return source.Where(finalPredicate);
    }

    // Helper method to combine two expressions with OR
    public static Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; Or&lt;T&gt;(
        this Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; expr1,
        Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; expr2)
    {
        var parameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T));

        var body = Expression.OrElse(
            Expression.Invoke(expr1, parameter),
            Expression.Invoke(expr2, parameter)
        );

        return Expression.Lambda&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt;(body, parameter);
    }

    public static Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; And&lt;T&gt;(
        this Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; expr1,
        Expression&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt; expr2)
    {
        var parameter = Expression.Parameter(typeof(T));
        var body = Expression.AndAlso(
            Expression.Invoke(expr1, parameter),
            Expression.Invoke(expr2, parameter)
        );
        return Expression.Lambda&lt;Func&lt;T, bool&gt;&gt;(body, parameter);
    }

    private static Expression&lt;Func&lt;TSource, bool&gt;&gt; BindSecondParameter&lt;TSource, TCompare&gt;(
        Expression&lt;Func&lt;TSource, TCompare, bool&gt;&gt; expr,
        TCompare value)
    {
        var sourceParam = expr.Parameters[0];
        var compareParam = expr.Parameters[1];

        var replacedBody = new ParameterReplaceVisitor(
            compareParam,
            Expression.Constant(value, typeof(TCompare))
        ).Visit(expr.Body);

        return Expression.Lambda&lt;Func&lt;TSource, bool&gt;&gt;(replacedBody, sourceParam);
    }

    internal class ParameterReplaceVisitor : ExpressionVisitor
    {
        private readonly ParameterExpression _parameter;
        private readonly Expression _replacement;

        public ParameterReplaceVisitor(ParameterExpression parameter, Expression replacement)
        {
            _parameter = parameter;
            _replacement = replacement;
        }

        protected override Expression VisitParameter(ParameterExpression node)
            =&gt; node == _parameter ? _replacement : base.VisitParameter(node);
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How can I combine these functions for different elements into one function? A: <p>Let's add an alternative: use a <a href="https://dev.to/matheusjulidori/do-you-know-how-it-works-javascript-factory-functions-4fh4" rel="nofollow noreferrer">factory function</a> to create small objects containing all you need to 'fly' a box (the <a href="https://depth-first.com/articles/2019/03/04/class-free-object-oriented-programming/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">class free object oriented</a> way).</p>
<p>Snippet code @<a href="https://codepen.io/kooiinc/pen/JoKaKQN" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Codepen</a>.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="false" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>flyBoxFactory("box1").fly();
flyBoxFactory("box2").fly();
flyBoxFactory("box3").fly();

function flyBoxFactory(boxId, distance) {
  const box = document.querySelector(`#${boxId}`);
  const initialLeft = box.getBoundingClientRect().left;
  let left = initialLeft, reverted = false;
  const maxLeft = initialLeft + (distance || 300);

  return Object.freeze({ fly });

  function move(revert) { 
    left += revert ? -1 : 1;
    box.style.left = `${left}px`;
  }

  function fly() {
    switch(true) {
      case left === initialLeft: reverted = false;
      case left &lt; maxLeft &amp;&amp; !reverted: move(reverted); break;
      case left &gt;= maxLeft || reverted: move(reverted = true);
    }

    return setTimeout(fly);
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.box {
  position: absolute; 
  left: 10px; 
  width: 50px; 
  height: 50px; 

  &amp;.b1 {
    top: 10px; 
    background-color: #aa39fc;
  }

  &amp;.b2 {
    top: 65px;
    background-color: #2c79f1;
  }

  &amp;.b3 {
    top: 120px;
    left: 60px;
    background-color: green;
  }
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div id="box1" class="box b1"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id="box2" class="box b2"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div id="box3" class="box b3"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Modify numeric values with Perl one-liner A: <p>Your data is well strcutured, so i don't see any benefit from using Regex here.</p>
<p>You can just parse the file, parse the lines, get first two items from each line, add 1 to them and write back.</p>
<p>In my opinion, here regex will be too implicit. I always prefer explicit implementation, if implicit option does not give me great benefits.</p>
<p>Here's simple example:</p>
<pre><code>#!/usr/bin/env perl
use strict;
use warnings;

my $data = &lt;&lt;'END_DATA';
0, 0, (1.263566e+02, -5.062154e+02)
0, 1, (1.069488e+02, -1.636887e+02)
0, 2, (-2.281294e-01, -7.787449e-01)
0, 3, (5.492424e+00, -4.145492e+01)
0, 4, (-7.961223e-01, 2.740912e+01)
END_DATA

for my $line (split /\n/, $data) {
    my @fields = split /, /, $line, 3;   # i, j, &quot;(real, imag)&quot;
    $fields[0]++;
    $fields[1]++;
    print join(&quot;, &quot;, @fields), &quot;\n&quot;;
}
</code></pre>
